---
description: strata_forge.llm — provider abstraction, tools, structured output, two-axis fallback, registry.
globs: ["src/strata_forge/llm/**/*.py", "src/strata_forge/llm/registry_data.yaml", "tests/unit/llm/**/*.py", "tests/vcr/**/*.py"]
alwaysApply: false
---

- Public surface is `LLMClient` (in `client.py`) plus the re-exports in `__init__.py`. Add new
  capabilities there, not in provider modules.
- Every provider call routes through LiteLLM via the `ProviderClient` base in `providers/base.py`.
  **Never import provider SDKs (`anthropic`, `openai`, `boto3`, Google's clients) outside
  `src/strata_forge/llm/providers/`** — the one exception is `errors.py`, which imports `openai` to
  name the exception classes LiteLLM re-raises. The caller-facing escape hatch is
  `provider_extras={...}` on `LLMClient.complete`, forwarded verbatim to LiteLLM.
- Provider faults raise `ProviderError` subclasses from `strata_forge.core.errors`; map them at the
  seam with `map_litellm_exception()` and never let a raw LiteLLM or SDK exception bubble out. Other
  module-level failures subclass `ForgeError` directly (`StructuredOutputError`,
  `ToolLoopExceededError`, `RegistryError`); argument mistakes stay `ValueError` / `TypeError`.
- Pricing and registry edits go in `registry_data.yaml` with the source link in a YAML comment.
  **Never hard-code pricing or model IDs in Python.** Registry scope is the latest Anthropic, OpenAI,
  and Google foundation models (ADR 0004) — a new vendor needs a superseding ADR.
- Cache keys are provider-agnostic: a hit on `claude-opus-4-7` is valid regardless of which provider
  route served the original call. Streaming bypasses both the cache and the fallback loop.
- Structured output dispatches by provider family: `response_format` on OpenAI-shaped routes,
  `response_schema` on Gemini, forced-tool emulation on Anthropic / Bedrock / Claude-on-Vertex. The
  payload is built once from the head of the chain, so a mixed-family fallback chain sends the head's
  shape downstream — keep chains within one family when you rely on structured output.
- `run_tool_loop` and `stream_tool_loop` gate on the registry before any provider call: a model whose
  entry says `capabilities.tool_calling` is false raises `RegistryError`, and an unregistered model
  is let through unless the client was built with `require_tool_support=True`.
- Fallback is two-axis: `ModelFallback(model, providers=[...])` fails over within one logical model
  (same model, different provider); the outer list fails over across models. A content-filter refusal
  short-circuits the entire chain — retrying it elsewhere only spends money (ADR 0005).
- Budget accounting is post-call: the client consumes against the active `BudgetContext` once a call
  returns, so a ceiling stops the next call rather than the one in flight. Never truncate a prompt to
  fit — raise `BudgetExceededError`.
- The NDJSON diagnostic dump is gated by `FORGE_DIAGNOSTIC_ENABLED` with the destination in
  `FORGE_DIAGNOSTIC_PATH` (`strata_forge.config.DiagnosticConfig`). There is no `FORGE_DIAGNOSTIC`
  variable.
- Tool plumbing (`Tool`, `@tool`, `ToolDeclaration`, the loop, the provider tool schemas) belongs to
  this module. `strata_forge.agents` composes it and must never reimplement it (ADR 0006, ADR 0011).
